\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1}Introduction}{1}{section.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The disagreement asymmetry oracle hypothesis: when a simple model and a complex model disagree, the direction of disagreement should predict which model is correct. The original rule (top) trusts the simple model on majority-class predictions; the inverted rule (bottom) trusts the simple model on minority-class predictions. Both rules achieve 49.0\% accuracy, indistinguishable from the majority-class prior.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fig1}{{1}{2}{The disagreement asymmetry oracle hypothesis: when a simple model and a complex model disagree, the direction of disagreement should predict which model is correct. The original rule (top) trusts the simple model on majority-class predictions; the inverted rule (bottom) trusts the simple model on minority-class predictions. Both rules achieve 49.0\% accuracy, indistinguishable from the majority-class prior}{figure.1}{}}
